= Solution
{parent=a}

Reduce $z$ using <modular congruence>: its <residue class> modulo $3$ is $0$, $1$, or $-1$. A <square number> therefore has <residue class> $0$ or $1$, and raising either residue to the positive <integer> power $n$ leaves it unchanged. Consequently
$$
\boxed{z^{2n}\equiv\begin{cases}0\pmod3,&3\mid z,\\1\pmod3,&3\nmid z.\end{cases}}
$$
The exponent in the original PDF is $2n$; the polynomial identities in the converted TeX are a transcription error. The argument uses $n\ge1$; it does not assign $0^0$ a value.

= b
{parent=1d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <Pythagorean triple>, each <square number> is $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$. If neither $x$ nor $y$ were divisible by $3$, <modular congruence> would give $z^2\equiv1+1\equiv2\pmod3$, impossible for a <square number>. Thus **at least one of $x,y$ is divisible by $3$**; both can be divisible, as in $(9,12,15)$.

An <odd number> has <square number> congruent to $1$ modulo $4$, whereas an <even number> has <square number> congruent to $0$. If both $x$ and $y$ were <odd numbers>, their sum of <square numbers> would again be $2$ modulo $4$, which no <square number> can be. Hence **$x$ and $y$ cannot both be odd**. They can both be even; the conclusion does not require the <Pythagorean triple> to be primitive.

